sections.s2_technical:
**The problem — IllegalStateException:**

```java
Stream<Trade> settled = trades.stream().filter(Trade::isSettled);

long count = settled.count();            // ✅ terminal #1 — consumed
List<Trade> list = settled.toList();     // ❌ IllegalStateException!
// "stream has already been operated upon or closed"

// Even intermediate after terminal fails:
// settled.map(Trade::getAmount);        // ❌ same exception!
```

**Why? The Spliterator cursor is exhausted:**

```java
// Internally, stream wraps a Spliterator:
Spliterator<Trade> split = trades.spliterator();
// After count() traverses all elements: split.tryAdvance() returns false
// The cursor cannot be rewound — no reset() method exists
// The stream is marked as CONSUMED (linkedOrConsumed flag = true)
```

**Workaround 1 — Supplier<Stream<T>> (RECOMMENDED):**

```java
// Store the RECIPE, not the stream:
Supplier<Stream<Trade>> settledStream =
    () -> trades.stream().filter(Trade::isSettled);

// Each .get() creates a FRESH stream:
long count = settledStream.get().count();              // fresh stream #1
List<Trade> list = settledStream.get().toList();       // fresh stream #2
BigDecimal total = settledStream.get()                 // fresh stream #3
    .map(Trade::getAmount)
    .reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add);

// Each call to .get() executes the lambda, creating:
//   trades.stream() → new Spliterator
//   .filter(Trade::isSettled) → new pipeline stage
// Completely independent streams — no shared state
```

**Workaround 2 — Collect to List first, then create new streams:**

```java
// Materialize once:
List<Trade> settledList = trades.stream()
    .filter(Trade::isSettled)
    .toList();  // one-time materialization

// Reuse the List (not the stream):
long count = settledList.size();                       // no stream needed
BigDecimal total = settledList.stream()                // new stream from List
    .map(Trade::getAmount).reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add);
Optional<Trade> first = settledList.stream()           // another new stream
    .findFirst();
```

**When to use which workaround:**

Supplier: when the source data might change between uses, or when you want to defer execution (lazy).

Collect-first: when data won't change and the filtered list is reasonably sized for memory.

